            "value": [
              {
                "dsDescriptionValue": {
                  "typeName": "dsDescriptionValue",
                  "multiple": false,
                  "typeClass": "primitive",
                  "value": "Study design\t\t\t\t\t\t In the Reijerscamp Natuurmonumenten established a large field experiment in 2006. There were four experimental areas (Area A-D). In each area large top-soil excavations were made down to the minaral soil (top-soil removal (rem)\r\n& untreated controls (cont)).\t\t\t In each of the excavated areas treatments were performed where 1) sods were added (sod), 2) where mixed soil was spead out (soil), and 3) a layer of hay was added (hay), and there was a control where nothing further had been done (cont) all three additions were collected from well-developped seminatural sites (sods: Doorwerthse Heide, Wolfheze, Soil: Dennenkamp, Planken Wambuis, Hay: Buurse Zand, Twente)\t\t\t Outside the excavations the soil, hay & control treatmen\r\nts were also performed just as inside the top-soil excavations. The sod addition treatment was not performed outside the top soil excavations (perhaps for financial reasons?)\t. Hence in total there were 4 experimental areas, with 7 treatments each, creating 28 sampling locations.\t\tWithin each sampling location a transect of 5 plots was laid out, with the centre plot in the middle of the sampling location. The orientation of the transect was decided randomly as far a the size of the treatment in each sampling location permitted (see tab 'transect orientation' in the primary data files).\t\t\t\t\t Each plot was 1x1m and the spacing between plot edges was 5 m (hence each transect from the far edges of plot 1\r\n& 5 spans 25 m).  All data in the primary data files were sampled in this way."
                },
                "dsDescriptionDate": {
                  "typeName": "dsDescriptionDate",
                  "multiple": false,
                  "typeClass": "primitive",
                  "value": "2014-07-15"
                }
              }
